  "executive_briefing": "As of mid-2026, the global corporate tax environment has entered a period of heightened volatility and defensive legislative positioning. While the OECD's Pillar Two framework has become a fixture for large multinational enterprises, new developments indicate a push-back in major jurisdictions, with some nations reconsidering adherence in response to mounting trade tensions. Concurrently, the global trade environment is defined by erratic and aggressive tariff policies, forcing a complete pivot from long-term supply chain efficiency to high-frequency, tax-adjusted cost modeling. Real-time digital enforcement, particularly through e-invoicing and transactional data transparency, is now the primary mechanism for governments to stabilize revenue in a fractured landscape.",
